WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.228
- In a way, The Martian is just

00:00:05.228 --> 00:00:06.853
The Hangover, in space,

00:00:06.853 --> 00:00:08.427
'cause they left a guy behind.

00:00:09.094 --> 00:00:11.857
(dramatic music)

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:14.579
One of the things that
they did really well

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:16.832
in the movie was the time it takes

00:00:16.832 --> 00:00:18.341
to communicate with Mars.

00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:21.359
- [Voiceover] We've got
an incoming message.

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:24.842
- Takes, like, 15 minutes
just to send a signal to Mars,

00:00:24.842 --> 00:00:27.210
and 15 minutes to get a signal back.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.779
- I'm a botanist.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:31.498
- The terraforming was
probably one of the most

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.494
accurate things in the movie.

00:00:33.494 --> 00:00:36.397
Growing potatoes with poop, martian soil,

00:00:36.397 --> 00:00:39.694
water from exploding rocket fuel,

00:00:39.694 --> 00:00:41.366
that is all totally feasible.

00:00:41.366 --> 00:00:42.364
- [Voiceover] Anyone gets lost,

00:00:42.364 --> 00:00:44.802
hone in to my suit's telemetry. You ready?

00:00:44.802 --> 00:00:45.406
- Ready.

00:00:45.406 --> 00:00:47.635
- The funny thing about
the tarp at the end

00:00:47.635 --> 00:00:50.468
was the movie kind of contradicted itself.

00:00:50.468 --> 00:00:52.371
It said oh, the
atmosphere's not that thick,

00:00:52.371 --> 00:00:54.206
we can just put a tarp on the top,

00:00:54.206 --> 00:00:55.761
but then there was a giant sand storm

00:00:55.761 --> 00:00:57.921
that knocked over a rocket
and impaled Matt Damon

00:00:57.921 --> 00:00:59.425
with a tiny spear.

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:01.171
So, which one is it, guys?

00:01:01.571 --> 00:01:02.805
- [Voiceover] Hey!

00:01:03.485 --> 00:01:04.407
- [Voiceover] Watch out!

00:01:04.407 --> 00:01:05.805
- Mars does have storms,

00:01:05.805 --> 00:01:07.756
but they're just not quite as violent

00:01:07.756 --> 00:01:09.172
as depicted in the movie.

00:01:09.172 --> 00:01:10.704
There is an atmosphere,

00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:14.071
there is wind, sometimes
the dust blows around,

00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:16.207
you get dust bunnies and what not,

00:01:16.207 --> 00:01:18.645
but there are not these
giant, violent storms

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.108
that can knock over a spaceship.

00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:22.035
I really loved the moment when they dug up

00:01:22.035 --> 00:01:24.055
the old Mars rover and used it to start

00:01:24.055 --> 00:01:26.238
communications back with the planet Earth.

00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:29.303
What a great call back to
stuff that JPL actually

00:01:29.303 --> 00:01:30.417
did in the nineties.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:31.996
- [Voiceover] 1200 kilometers in diameter

00:01:31.996 --> 00:01:34.156
bearing 24.41 degrees.

00:01:34.156 --> 00:01:37.035
- It was really nice to
see some females in lead

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:39.403
and important roles in this movie.

00:01:39.403 --> 00:01:42.398
I think there's a lot of really fantastic

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:45.649
real life female role
models in the space program,

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:47.785
and in other places throughout NASA.

00:01:47.785 --> 00:01:49.503
We need more, but it's nice to see them

00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:50.290
on the big screen.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.808
- [Voiceover] If we mess up
the Earth gravity assist,

00:01:54.509 --> 00:01:54.981
we die.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:56.630
- Lots of people think it's
time to start trying to

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:58.859
colonize other planets like Mars,

00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:00.995
and it may be important
to begin to explore

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:02.411
and look to those places,

00:02:02.411 --> 00:02:03.781
but you can't forget that we have

00:02:03.781 --> 00:02:04.826
to take care of the planet

00:02:04.826 --> 00:02:06.475
we already live on.

00:02:06.475 --> 00:02:09.563
We're not going to get all 7
billion people off the planet

00:02:09.563 --> 00:02:11.699
to Mars, and in the meantime,

00:02:11.699 --> 00:02:13.928
ours is kinda getting devastated.

00:02:13.928 --> 00:02:16.064
So, pay attention to home,

00:02:16.064 --> 00:02:17.945
but look to the stars too.

00:02:17.945 --> 00:02:20.424
(dramatic music)

